From dcbbb12202f9c43038b93ae5f3ea0b330ff8a02a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lmoresi <louis.moresi@anu.edu.au>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 01:29:20 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Testing guide: bound iteration counts, never assert them
 exactly

An iteration count is reproducible only to round-off, from run to run on one
build and in serial too. PETSc's VecMDot takes the BLAS gemv path when the
vectors happen to be adjacent in memory, and its own loops otherwise
(-vec_mdot_use_gemv, on by default), and allocation placement picks the path.
On test_1157's low-viscosity case the last velocity sub-solve took 78
iterations or ran to its cap of 200 with the solution the same to 16 digits.
With -vec_mdot_use_gemv 0 the runs were bit-identical (UNFILED 64).

Underworld development team with AI support from Claude Code
---
 docs/developer/TESTING-RELIABILITY-SYSTEM.md | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/developer/TESTING-RELIABILITY-SYSTEM.md b/docs/developer/TESTING-RELIABILITY-SYSTEM.md
index a6d6f5ee..53276010 100644
--- a/docs/developer/TESTING-RELIABILITY-SYSTEM.md
+++ b/docs/developer/TESTING-RELIABILITY-SYSTEM.md
@@ -300,6 +300,17 @@ pytest -m "not tier_c"
 3. **Use xfail Appropriately**: If testing unimplemented feature, mark with xfail
 4. **Don't Break CI**: Tier C tests with xfail won't break automated testing
 5. **Promote Deliberately**: Tier B → A is the review step described above
+6. **Bound iteration counts; never assert them exactly**: an iteration count,
+   outer or sub-solve, is reproducible only to round-off, from run to run on one
+   build and in serial too. PETSc computes the multiple dot products in GMRES and
+   FGMRES orthogonalisation through BLAS `gemv` when the vectors happen to be
+   adjacent in memory, and through its own loops otherwise (`-vec_mdot_use_gemv`,
+   on by default), and allocation placement picks the path. A sub-solve that
+   stagnates near its target turns the last-bit difference into a different count.
+   On `test_1157`'s low-viscosity case the last velocity sub-solve took 78
+   iterations or ran to its cap of 200, while the solution agreed to 16 digits
+   (UNFILED 64). Assert an upper bound tight enough to catch a regression, and
+   assert the answer against its baseline.
 
 ### When a Test Fails
 
